<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Bandar Rig</h1><p class="st-meta">1730 CE – 1769 CE (approx.) · capital Bandar Rig</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Arab-ruled port on the Persian coast of the Gulf, notorious under Mir Muhanna for piracy and the seizure of Kharg island from the Dutch.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-rig">Bandar Rig</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a>, was ruled in the 18th century by the Za'ab, an Arab family settled on the Persian shore. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747 central authority collapsed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-nasir-of-bandar-rig">Mir Nasir</a> governed the port as a local lord with links to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> on nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharg-island">Kharg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lordship lived from fishing, trade and raiding. In 1753 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-nasir-of-bandar-rig">Mir Nasir</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhanna">Mir Muhanna</a> killed his father and took power. He became the most feared pirate of the northern Gulf, attacking shipping and coastal towns. In 1765 he drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> out of their fortified trading post on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharg-island">Kharg</a> and occupied the island.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire</a> sent forces against him, and in 1769 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhanna">Mir Muhanna</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, where he was executed by the Ottoman authorities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-rig">Bandar Rig</a> came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand</a> control.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1730 CE – 1769 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.13%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1730 CE – 1769 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bandar Rig</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mir Nasir, Mir Muhanna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bandar Rig, Za'ab chiefdom of Bandar Rig</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1750&amp;focus=lordship-of-bandar-rig" aria-label="Lordship of Bandar Rig on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5998 1100 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Bandar Rig lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6318 1356L6364 1269"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6364 1269h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6318 1356h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6318 1356h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1750&amp;focus=lordship-of-bandar-rig">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

